WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000 align:start position:0% line:0%
Transcriber: Leslie Gauthier

00:00:04.834 --> 00:00:07.963
We live in a time-pressed culture.

00:00:08.547 --> 00:00:10.590
There is never enough time.

00:00:11.132 --> 00:00:12.425
And we see it,

00:00:12.425 --> 00:00:14.594
we feel it around us every day.

00:00:15.554 --> 00:00:19.182
We live in a world that valorizes work,

00:00:19.182 --> 00:00:20.850
accomplishment,

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:22.060
busyness.

00:00:22.435 --> 00:00:23.979
And there’s real upside to that;

00:00:23.979 --> 00:00:25.146
there’s real value.

00:00:25.355 --> 00:00:26.356
We’re pushed,

00:00:26.356 --> 00:00:30.860
we’re driven toward
achievement and action and creation.

00:00:31.152 --> 00:00:32.737
And that’s great,

00:00:32.737 --> 00:00:34.656
but there’s also a downside.

00:00:35.323 --> 00:00:38.660
And that's something that I think
is worth talking about.

00:00:39.035 --> 00:00:40.787
There was a study done a while back,

00:00:40.787 --> 00:00:42.372
by the Management Research Group,

00:00:42.372 --> 00:00:44.749
of 10,000 senior leaders.

00:00:45.208 --> 00:00:46.418
And they asked them,

00:00:46.418 --> 00:00:49.963
“What is key to your
organization’s success?”

00:00:50.255 --> 00:00:55.927
And 97 percent said
long-term strategic thinking.

00:00:56.303 --> 00:01:00.473
I mean, when was the last time that
97 percent of people agreed on anything?

00:01:00.932 --> 00:01:05.228
There is near unanimity
that being a long-term thinker --

00:01:05.228 --> 00:01:06.354
having perspective,

00:01:06.354 --> 00:01:09.399
having the ability to think
and ask big questions --

00:01:09.399 --> 00:01:11.818
is essential to our success.

00:01:12.277 --> 00:01:14.154
And yet in a separate study,

00:01:14.154 --> 00:01:17.991
96 percent of leaders were surveyed,

00:01:17.991 --> 00:01:21.661
and they said they don’t have time
for strategic thinking.

00:01:21.661 --> 00:01:22.662
(Laughter)

00:01:22.871 --> 00:01:24.414
What is going on?

00:01:24.831 --> 00:01:25.832
Why is it --

00:01:25.832 --> 00:01:27.375
how can it be

00:01:27.375 --> 00:01:32.672
that 96 percent of people
are not doing the one thing

00:01:32.672 --> 00:01:36.426
that they say is most critical
to their success?

00:01:37.302 --> 00:01:39.721
Well, I think we know the answer ...

00:01:39.721 --> 00:01:41.473
or at least we think we do.

00:01:41.765 --> 00:01:46.353
The average professional
attends 62 meetings per month.

00:01:46.478 --> 00:01:48.605
That sounds pretty outrageous.

00:01:48.647 --> 00:01:49.648
How could that be?

00:01:49.898 --> 00:01:51.608
But if you actually break it down,

00:01:51.608 --> 00:01:52.609
it’s not that many.

00:01:52.609 --> 00:01:54.736
It’s two to three meetings per day,

00:01:54.736 --> 00:01:56.905
which is probably average for many of you.

00:01:57.364 --> 00:01:58.907
So 62 meetings a month.

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:00.575
That does not help,

00:02:00.575 --> 00:02:02.410
and that is not wrong.

00:02:02.744 --> 00:02:03.828
It is a contributor.

00:02:04.287 --> 00:02:05.872
Also, we know --

00:02:05.872 --> 00:02:07.916
we know what else ...

00:02:07.916 --> 00:02:08.917
email.

00:02:08.917 --> 00:02:10.794
A study a while back by McKinsey

00:02:10.794 --> 00:02:15.757
showed that the average professional
spends 28 percent of their time

00:02:15.757 --> 00:02:17.300
just responding to email.

00:02:17.842 --> 00:02:19.511
Of course that drains us,

00:02:19.511 --> 00:02:21.930
of course that makes us busy.

00:02:22.514 --> 00:02:26.267
But the truth is, it’s also,
I believe, not the full picture.

00:02:26.685 --> 00:02:28.561
Those are manifestations.

00:02:28.561 --> 00:02:30.772
Those are problems, legitimately.

00:02:31.106 --> 00:02:35.652
But there are also some other things
going on underneath the surface,

00:02:35.652 --> 00:02:38.530
reasons that perhaps we are,

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:39.739
in some ways,

00:02:39.739 --> 00:02:42.117
working at cross-purposes.

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:43.618
Because for so long

00:02:43.618 --> 00:02:49.582
almost all of us have said
we want desperately to be less busy,

00:02:49.582 --> 00:02:54.629
and yet we keep making choices
that put ourselves in the position

00:02:54.629 --> 00:02:56.923
where we’re just as busy
as we’ve always been.

00:02:57.424 --> 00:02:59.676
What is going on?

00:03:00.135 --> 00:03:03.096
Well, some research
out of Columbia University

00:03:03.096 --> 00:03:04.848
sheds a little bit of light on this.

00:03:05.098 --> 00:03:08.643
Silvia Bellezza and her colleagues
have done interesting research

00:03:08.643 --> 00:03:10.645
into the fact that in some cultures --

00:03:10.645 --> 00:03:12.939
American culture chief among them --

00:03:12.939 --> 00:03:16.192
busyness is actually a form of status.

00:03:16.443 --> 00:03:21.156
When we say, “Oh, I am so crazy busy,”

00:03:21.156 --> 00:03:22.907
what we’re really saying

00:03:22.907 --> 00:03:28.371
is a societally-accepted version
of “I am so important --

00:03:28.371 --> 00:03:29.372
(Laughter)

00:03:29.372 --> 00:03:30.582
“I am so popular!

00:03:30.582 --> 00:03:32.083
I am so in demand!”

00:03:32.959 --> 00:03:39.174
And the truth is that feeling
can be hard to give up ...

00:03:39.174 --> 00:03:40.925
even if we say that we want to.

00:03:41.634 --> 00:03:43.470
That’s not the only reason, of course.

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:46.264
It turns out it is very hard

00:03:46.264 --> 00:03:50.477
for the human mind to deal
with conditions of uncertainty.

00:03:50.769 --> 00:03:52.729
And in modern life, there’s a lot of it.

00:03:53.313 --> 00:03:57.859
Sometimes we are given
tasks or challenges,

00:03:57.859 --> 00:04:01.196
and the truth is, tactically,
we just don’t know how to do it.

00:04:01.529 --> 00:04:03.364
“Increase sales by 30 percent.”

00:04:03.782 --> 00:04:05.366
Well, how?

00:04:05.992 --> 00:04:07.827
There’s a lot of ways you could do it.

00:04:07.827 --> 00:04:08.870
You’re not sure how.

00:04:08.870 --> 00:04:12.540
Sometimes it’s easier, frankly,
to just double down

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:15.627
and keep doing more
of what you’re already doing.

00:04:16.211 --> 00:04:18.755
That might not be the best answer,

00:04:18.755 --> 00:04:20.298
but it’s an answer,

00:04:20.298 --> 00:04:21.800
and it removes uncertainty.

00:04:22.509 --> 00:04:25.386
The picture gets even worse

00:04:25.386 --> 00:04:29.057
when we’re talking
about existential questions;

00:04:29.057 --> 00:04:33.019
when we’re talking
about uncomfortable matters

00:04:33.019 --> 00:04:36.856
that we might not actually
really want to deal with.

00:04:37.524 --> 00:04:40.276
That might be, “Am I in the right job?”

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:44.030
It might be, “Am I in the right career?”

00:04:44.864 --> 00:04:46.866
Those are often questions,

00:04:46.866 --> 00:04:48.034
truth be told,

00:04:48.034 --> 00:04:49.869
we might not want the answer to.

00:04:50.495 --> 00:04:52.288
And so we become busy

00:04:52.288 --> 00:04:55.583
as a way so that we don’t
even have to ask the question.

00:04:56.334 --> 00:04:58.086
Now, there's a third reason,

00:04:58.086 --> 00:05:02.966
and I’ll admit it’s one
that I know well, personally,

00:05:02.966 --> 00:05:08.805
and that is that sometimes we use busyness
as a way to numb ourselves out.

00:05:09.305 --> 00:05:10.557
I’ve experienced that.

00:05:10.890 --> 00:05:13.309
This is my boy Gideon,

00:05:13.309 --> 00:05:16.479
and he died in 2013.

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:19.065
I’d had him for 17 years,

00:05:19.065 --> 00:05:20.650
and he was my best friend.

00:05:21.109 --> 00:05:23.403
And after he died,

00:05:23.403 --> 00:05:24.404
I’ll be honest,

00:05:24.404 --> 00:05:26.781
I didn’t want to be home

00:05:26.781 --> 00:05:29.409
because I knew that he wouldn’t be there.

00:05:30.118 --> 00:05:33.329
And so for two years,

00:05:33.329 --> 00:05:38.084
my life basically
was an Uber to an airport,

00:05:38.084 --> 00:05:39.836
to a hotel

00:05:39.836 --> 00:05:41.087
and back again,

00:05:41.087 --> 00:05:43.923
because I just really didn’t
want to face that.

00:05:44.424 --> 00:05:47.719
For a lot of us, there are things
we sometimes don’t want to face.

00:05:48.136 --> 00:05:52.599
What we’re really looking for
with work is an anesthetic.

00:05:53.308 --> 00:05:54.350
And as I like to say,

00:05:54.350 --> 00:05:57.061
work is better than crack --

00:05:57.061 --> 00:05:58.062
(Laughter)

00:05:58.062 --> 00:05:59.647
so if you’re choosing ...

00:05:59.647 --> 00:06:01.399
(Laughter)

00:06:01.399 --> 00:06:02.483
it’s not the worst.

00:06:02.692 --> 00:06:04.736
(Laughter)

00:06:04.986 --> 00:06:08.907
But the truth is, it's also not
a sustainable solution.

00:06:09.365 --> 00:06:14.454
For many of us, we get trapped
in the pattern of busyness,

00:06:14.454 --> 00:06:16.080
of overwork.

00:06:16.414 --> 00:06:20.501
It's hard sometimes even to remember
what it was like before.

00:06:21.336 --> 00:06:23.296
Oftentimes in our mind’s eye,

00:06:23.296 --> 00:06:25.340
when we think of busyness,

00:06:25.340 --> 00:06:27.008
what we think of is this.

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:30.011
What we think of is triumphant success

00:06:30.011 --> 00:06:32.263
and the world at your fingertips.

00:06:33.139 --> 00:06:36.643
The truth is, more often,
busyness looks like this.

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:39.646
It looks like loneliness.

00:06:40.021 --> 00:06:41.856
It looks like frustration.

00:06:42.231 --> 00:06:45.610
It looks like having a life

00:06:45.610 --> 00:06:48.446
that’s not really in your full control.

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:53.117
So I would like to propose
that we make a change.

00:06:53.785 --> 00:06:57.497
Because if we are ever going to succeed

00:06:57.497 --> 00:07:02.543
in beating back busyness once and for all,

00:07:02.543 --> 00:07:05.546
first of all, we have to get real

00:07:05.546 --> 00:07:10.760
and acknowledge what is actually
behind some of the busyness

00:07:10.760 --> 00:07:13.930
that is filling our days.

00:07:14.597 --> 00:07:19.268
We have to really get honest
about what it is that’s motivating us

00:07:19.268 --> 00:07:21.479
so that we can make a different choice.

00:07:21.479 --> 00:07:24.482
Because it is about our choice.

00:07:25.358 --> 00:07:30.905
We need to recognize that real freedom
is about creating the space

00:07:30.905 --> 00:07:32.198
so that we can breathe,

00:07:32.198 --> 00:07:34.450
the space so that we can think.

00:07:35.576 --> 00:07:39.414
Ultimately, real freedom
is about choosing how

00:07:39.414 --> 00:07:42.500
and with whom we want
to be spending our time.

00:07:43.292 --> 00:07:44.293
Thank you.

00:07:44.293 --> 00:07:45.294
(Cheers)

00:07:45.294 --> 00:07:46.295
(Applause)

